[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m 4. In both cases, **$ t $** is the time spent **in the coffee shop** (in minutes), and the rest of the time is **walking time**.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m 5. We are to find the time (in **minutes**) it takes her to walk **9 km** at a speed of **$ s + \frac{1}{2} $** km/h, **including** the $ t $ minutes in the coffee shop.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m ---
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m ### Step 1: Let’s translate the information into equations.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m Let’s denote:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m - $ t $ = time spent in the coffee shop (in **minutes**)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m - $ T $ = total time (in **hours**) = 4 hours (for the first case)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m So, the **walking time** (excluding coffee shop) is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m $$
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m \text{Walking time} = T - \frac{t}{60} = 4 - \frac{t}{60}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m $$
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m The **walking speed** is $ s $ km/h, so the **walking time** is also:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m $$
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m \frac{9}{s} = 4 - \frac{t}{60}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m $$
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m So, we get the first equation:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m $$
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m \frac{9}{s} = 4 - \frac{t}{60} \tag{1}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m $$
