[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m ## Step 3: The "Expected Time Until All Snakes Merge"
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m This is a well-known problem in probability and combinatorics. The setup is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m - A circle of $ n $ points.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m - $ m $ initial "points" (here: snakes).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m - Each point moves left or right with probability $ \frac{1}{2} $ each second.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m - Points merge when they touch (i.e., are adjacent).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m - We are to find the **expected time until all points merge into one**.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m This is known as the **"coalescence" problem on a circle**.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m There is a known formula for this expected time.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m > **Theorem:**  
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m > If there are $ m $ points on a circle of $ n $ positions, each moving randomly either clockwise or counterclockwise with probability $ \frac{1}{2} $, then the **expected time until all points merge** is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m >
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m > $$
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m > T = \frac{n}{2} \binom{m}{2}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m > $$
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m So in our case:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m - $ n = 45 $ (circle has 45 arcs)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m - $ m = 15 $ (initial number of snakes, i.e., points)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m - So the expected time is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m $$
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m T = \frac{45}{2} \binom{15}{2} = \frac{45}{2} \cdot \frac{15 \cdot 14}{2} = \frac{45}{2} \cdot 105 = \frac{4725}{2} = 2362.5
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m $$
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m ---
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m ## Step 4: Final Answer
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m Thus, the **expected number of seconds until only one snake remains** is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m $$
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m \boxed{2362.5}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m $$
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=74714)[0m This matches the expected result of the standard "coalescence on a circle" problem.
